ch harder ask than the calculator was. This is the second thing I run when I'm putting an agent through its paces inside Agent OS, because the calculator tells you if it can build, and the landing page tells you if it can structure. Same rule as before. One prompt, no follow-ups. Now, here's the part I promised you, and it's the piece that makes Agent OS different. Inside Agent OS, there's a shared memory vault that every agent can reach. It's powered by an Obsidian vault, which means your notes, your contacts, and your past work live in plain files you own, not locked inside one app's chat history. So, when you use an agent inside Agent OS, it isn't meeting you for the first time. It already has the context from every other time you've used them. Without that, every new agent means re-explaining your brand, your voice, and your audience over and over. With it, you brief once. The writing agent knows what the research agent found. The build agent knows what the writing agent wrote. That's where the real time saving shows up. One more thing before you try it. Bots don't only talk in group rooms. Every bot chat carries a messaging tool, so a bot can send a teammate a message by name. It writes its own message, and the reply comes back attributed to that agent. Every bot knows the roster, names, and roles, so it knows who does what before it picks who to ask. Delivery is fire and forget, which means the receiving bot picks the message up the next time it runs, rather than answering live. If you want to go deeper than watching me do it, come join us in the AI Profit Boardroom. Here's the thing about multi-agent setups. The first day is fun. Then you hit the real questions. Which agent should own which job? How do you stop two of them duplicating work? What do you do when one machine won't connect? That's exactly what the Boardroom is built for. You get the Agent OS zip file ready to install, the 30-day roadmap that goes with it, the walkthroughs, the live coaching calls